import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Analyzer from "../components/Analyzer.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Cipher identifier — analyze unknown ciphertext online"
  description="Paste unknown ciphertext to identify the cipher: index of coincidence, key length, entropy, and autokey detection. Runs in your browser."
>
  <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">What cipher is this?</h1>
  <p class="mb-8 max-w-prose text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
    Paste ciphertext and this tool measures its statistics to suggest which
    classical cipher produced it, and its likely key length. Nothing is sent to
    a server.
  </p>
  <Analyzer />

  <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    How identification works
  </h2>
  <div
    class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"
  >
    <p>
      The index of coincidence measures how often two letters drawn from the
      text match. English text scores about 1.73 (normalized), random text about
      1.0. A monoalphabetic cipher — Caesar or substitution — keeps the English
      score because it only relabels letters. A repeating-key cipher flattens
      the score toward 1.0, but the score rebounds when the text is split into
      columns at the key length, which is how the key length is found.
    </p>
    <p>
      Autokey ciphers, where the message itself extends the key, defeat that
      column trick because the key never repeats. A ciphertext autokey still
      leaves a tell: subtracting each letter from the one a primer-length
      earlier reconstructs the plaintext, so that difference reads as English.
      Plaintext autokey hides even this, and is the hardest of the family to
      spot.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
